  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on self-reported perception surveys and qualitative observations from two specific neighborhoods, which may limit direct transferability to other urban West African contexts.",
      "what": "A comparative survey of 226 household heads across East Legon and Kwabenya in Greater Accra evaluated drivers and perceptions of domestic gardening using Mann-Whitney U tests and the Relative Importance Index. While homeowners across both areas consistently recognized ecological functions like microclimate regulation, flood mitigation, and soil enrichment, perceptions of healthy eating (r = 0.804), poverty reduction (r = 0.490), and sustainable technology adoption (r = 0.316) varied significantly. Observational evidence confirmed Kwabenya's gardens lean toward food production, whereas East Legon features more ornamental, technology-supported spaces.",
      "why": "The study provides empirical evidence from sub-Saharan Africa demonstrating how household socioeconomics shape the distinct functional roles of private residential green space, offering a basis to integrate private gardens into urban climate adaptation strategies."
    }
  },
